{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/workroom as main carries it, at merge commit e677dd8f54f1380040e19a36a290f7a32f7841d5, which landed the reviewed-paths work approved by codex in 66e46023 and ratified in 4c64bd4d.\n\nThe fold now recognises the set of paths an approval actually reviewed, rather than the single path named in the verdict body. It reads every artifact the approval rests on that stands at the reviewed head and shares the implementer, and a merge may retire a pointer on any of those paths. Before this, a head touching three paths could only retire another actor pointers on the one path its body named, which is what refused this very merge on its first attempt.\n\nThis replaces f99e1990, published by gs merge for this same commit and stale from birth for the reason recorded in the accompanying assert: the automation rests each successor on the merge receipt, whose chain runs back through the approval to the candidate artifacts that the same batch retires.\n\nSuccession at this path is otherwise complete and was done by the machinery, not by hand. It retired 24279d39, 2f5f4a4b, b17d63aa, 85422ca6, and both 0d7b8dde and b67e9b56 — the two pointers eleven pages and one page cite, which had stood as debt because the old rule refused any cited retirement. Here they were retired with a successor covering the path, so the citing pages flare and are told where to re-anchor instead of being orphaned. That is the case the cited-with-successor rule was written for, and this is its first live exercise.\n\nIt rests on 9891ec40 at internal/gitstore, live and not stale, at a path this merge does not touch.","body":{"commit":"e677dd8f54f1380040e19a36a290f7a32f7841d5","path":"internal/workroom"}}